<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hakata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hakata is a historic port on Hakata Bay in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, now a ward of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a>. For centuries it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main gateway for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and the Mongol invasions of 1274 and 1281 landed nearby. Its merchants prospered in the Ming tally trade under the Ōuchi, and in 1601 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> began Fukuoka castle beside the merchant town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hakata.jpg" alt="Hakata" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZENsan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hakata_Port_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hakata lies on the eastern side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a>, on low plains beside the Mikasa River, with Hakata Bay to the north-west. It faces the Genkai-Nada, the channel that divides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, which made it a natural base for the sea trade with the continent. The Naka River separates the old merchant town from the opposite shore, where the castle town of Fukuoka grew. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> old Hakata covered no more than a square kilometre, bounded by defensive lines along the Naka River, the Boshu Canal and the Ishido or Mikasa River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hakata is one of the oldest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a base for merchants trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and held Japan's first Chinatown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> is said to have built the artificial harbour of Sode-no-minato to increase its commerce. The town was burned in many wars, including the Mongol invasions. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> became lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikuzen-province">Chikuzen Province</a>, most of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> lived across the Naka River at Fukusaki, which he renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> after his hometown; he built Fukuoka Castle with stones from the dismantled Tachibana and Najima castles.</p><p>Hakata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> were merged in 1876, and in 1878 the prefecture named the joint settlement Fukuoka-ku, although Hakata had 25,677 people to Fukuoka's 20,410. In 1889 a vote split evenly between the two names; the city became Fukuoka, but the new railway station was called Hakata Station. An imperial decree of July 1899 opened the port to trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom, and in 1972 the ward covering old Hakata was named Hakata-ku.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hakata-ku holds the Fukuoka Prefectural Office, Hakata Station and Fukuoka Airport. The old Hakata area is still the centre of Hakata ningyō doll making, the town gives its name to Hakata-ori textile, and its festivals include the Hakata Gion Yamakasa in July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hakata-ku, Fukuoka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Kublai again demanded submission, the Hōjō had the messengers executed and prepared: a wall was built round the hinterland of <b>Hakata</b> Bay, defensive posts and garrison lists were set up and ships were built to harass the invaders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He built Fukuoka castle beside the old merchant city of <b>Hakata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukuoka-domain">Fukuoka Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>They dominated the official tally trade with Ming China after 1523 and traded with Joseon, using the port of <b>Hakata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouchi-clan-domain">Ouchi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
